 "autor": "claude",
 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Why does a well that worked for years suddenly pull up air in August?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The well didn't break. The water underground moved.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Under every farm there's a water table, where the soil is soaked like a sponge.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A well works only if its bottom sits below that line.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In spring, rain soaks in and the water table rises.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In summer, there's little rain, and the sun and plants drink the topsoil dry.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A single big tree can pull hundreds of liters of water a day out of the ground.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then everyone starts pumping more for gardens and animals.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Each pump sucks down a cone of dry soil around its well.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The water table sinks below the bottom of shallow wells.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The bucket comes up empty, even though the ground still holds water deeper down.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why old farmers dug wells at the end of summer, when the water was lowest.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Modern wells go much deeper, into rock layers called aquifers.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In California's twenty twenty-two drought, more than a thousand household wells went dry.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So if your well runs dry, don't panic. It usually comes back with the autumn rain.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
